"""Phase 4e -- Appendices (target -20%). Protected tables untouched (G5 tab:deeparch, D19
tab:d17label, tab:basestats). Appendix C keeps both labels side by side, per-run
differences and effective n (D19). Internal contradiction resolved: C's first paragraph
said the original label made test 3 'a guaranteed 100% miss for every detector', while its
third paragraph (and Table d17label) show six of ten -- now 'most detectors', with the
exact six stated in the third paragraph. N-29 corrected 75.6 is kept in D.1.
"""
from p4lib import Doc

d = Doc()
P = d.para
P("Table~\\ref{tab:hyperparams} lists the fixed hyperparameters used throughout",
  "Table~\\ref{tab:hyperparams}a lists the fixed hyperparameters, taken verbatim from the released config.py, and Table~\\ref{tab:deeparch} the architectures and training protocol of the four deep models, with trainable parameter counts measured at the 49-dimensional invariant feature width; one protocol applies to every detector, dataset, run and sampling factor, with no per-condition tuning. Table~\\ref{tab:hyperparams}b gives the wall-clock training and per-window inference cost on the largest IMS run ($X_{\\text{train}}=631$, $X_{\\text{test}}=506$ windows, single CPU core, median of five repeats). Training is a one-off offline step, and inference is at most ${\\sim}0.5$~ms per window (LSTM-AE), more than four orders of magnitude below the fastest historian polling interval in our study (10~s), so streaming condition monitoring at native SCADA rates is not compute-bound for any of the eleven detectors.")
P("Each snapshot's raw waveform yields six per-channel",
  "Each snapshot's raw waveform yields the six per-channel time-domain statistics of Table~\\ref{tab:basestats}. Each family is made channel-count-invariant by the channel mean and the channel max, and each aggregated series is summarised over the rolling window (10 samples, 50\\% overlap) by its mean, standard deviation, maximum and least-squares trend slope, giving $6 \\times 2 \\times 4 = 48$ features; a cross-channel RMS-coherence feature (the mean pairwise Pearson correlation of the physical RMS channels within the window) brings the total to 49. A train-fit variance top-$k$ selection ($k \\le 50$) keeps $p < n$, every statistic is fit on the training split only, and feature naming and the exact construction are in the released code.")
P("A subtlety we correct here is the failure-label of the third test:",
  "An earlier label placed the failure of the third IMS test on 2004-04-08, leaving 21.5\\% of the recorded data after the nominal ``failure''---impossible for a run-to-failure series. We relabel it to the last recorded sample (2004-04-18 02:42), at which the mean-RMS health indicator has ramped from its flat baseline (${\\sim}0.068$) to ${\\sim}0.228$ before the final machine-off reading: within the flat normal-region baseline band at the old label, but well above the $4\\sigma$ onset band at the corrected one. The relabeling moves the third test toward harder, not easier, detection: a run that was a guaranteed miss for most detectors becomes evaluable, and because the aggregate-vs-decimate contrast is computed within each run, the relabeling cannot artificially inflate it. It affects only the single hardest run and is not a tuning choice. We present it as an author relabeling based on the health-indicator trajectory, not independently verified ground truth: we know of no published benchmark that corrects this label, so we do not claim it as a community-established correction. It is the labeling we use throughout.")
P("\\emph{The relabeling made our own claim harder to support, not easier.}",
  "\\emph{The relabeling made our own claim harder to support, not easier.} Because a reader may ask whether the label was chosen for the result it produces, we re-ran the entire IMS sweep under the original 2004-04-08 label and report both arms side by side (Table~\\ref{tab:d17label}). The decisive case is $3\\sigma$: under the original label the third test is a guaranteed miss, both lead times are zero, the run's collapsed difference is exactly $0.00$~h, and the zero-difference rule of Section~\\ref{sec:statproto} leaves $3\\sigma$ at two positive and zero negative runs. An earlier version of this work claimed that in no run does aggregation shorten the lead time of the magnitude-monitoring detectors. That claim would have been \\emph{true} under the original label; it is false only under the corrected one, where the third test contributes $-1.00$~h, and we have withdrawn it. The relabeling moved the evidence against the paper's own claim rather than in its favor, the opposite of what a result-motivated relabeling would do.")
P("\\emph{The effective sample size is detector-dependent",
  "\\emph{The effective sample size is detector-dependent, and neither label can reach significance.} Because the zero-difference exclusion operates per detector, the original label does not simply reduce IMS to $n=2$: it leaves $n_{\\text{eff}}=3$ for the four detectors that still warn on the third test, $n_{\\text{eff}}=2$ for $3\\sigma$, CUSUM, EWMA and Isolation Forest, and $n_{\\text{eff}}=1$ for Deep SVDD and RMS-trend. The exact two-sided sign test floors at $p=0.25$, $0.50$ and $1.00$ at those sample sizes, so under the original label six of the ten detectors could not have reached $\\alpha=0.05$ at any effect size, and the four with $n_{\\text{eff}}=3$ floor at $p=0.25$, already above $\\alpha$. The corrected label retains every run, but $n=3$ still bounds the attainable $p$ at $0.25$, so no IMS detector reaches significance under either label and the best attainable $p$ is $0.25$ in both arms (Hotelling $T^2$). The label does not change the direction: no median flips sign between the arms, $3\\sigma$ holding at $+15.10$~h and Hotelling $T^2$ at $+1.17$~h, and the Holm verdict of Section~\\ref{sec:results} and the headline are untouched.")
P("The IMS trend admits a clean candidate mechanism:",
  "If bin-averaging helps on IMS because it is a low-pass filter suppressing single-window noise in the health statistic, letting the alarm threshold be crossed earlier and held more persistently than under decimation, two predictions follow. We test both on IMS at sampling factor 5, controlling window content so that only the smoothing differs.")
P("\\emph{Prediction 1: the aggregate advantage should grow with noise.}",
  "\\emph{Prediction 1: the aggregate advantage should grow with noise.} We add zero-mean Gaussian noise to the per-window health statistic at signal-to-noise ratios of 30, 20 and 10~dB and recompute the aggregate$-$decimate lead time, collapsing the four magnitude-monitoring SPC charts to one mean difference per run ($n=3$ per level) to avoid the pseudoreplication corrected in the main analysis. The mean advantage rises monotonically with noise (Table~\\ref{tab:mechanism}a), from $-0.35$~h at the native noise level to $+6.6$~h at 10~dB, and at the two highest noise levels all three runs share the positive sign. The lone large negative, the $-7.94$~h native-noise entry, is IMS test~1, the abrupt near-coincident-onset run whose degradation begins only ${\\sim}12$~h before failure, which also violates the conformal guarantee (Figure~\\ref{fig:conformal}a) and is unwarnable under every feature set. This is the qualitative signature of a low-pass mechanism, the noisier the signal the more bin-averaging helps, but at $n=3$ it is a directional trend, not a significant effect (the exact sign test floors at $p=0.25$, and at 30~dB the per-run signs are mixed): it corroborates the mechanism and cannot, at this sample size, establish it statistically.")
P("\\emph{Prediction 2: any smoother on the decimated stream should recover the advantage.}",
  "\\emph{Prediction 2: any smoother on the decimated stream should recover the advantage.} We compare a median filter, a moving average, a constant-velocity Kalman filter and a wavelet denoiser, each applied to the decimated stream, against raw aggregation and raw decimation (Table~\\ref{tab:mechanism}b). The moving average and Kalman filter recover essentially the full aggregate lead time (76.0 and 75.6~h vs.\\ aggregation's 75.6~h), while the median and wavelet filters fall slightly short, so the effect is generic smoothing of the health signal, not a property unique to historian bin-averaging: an operator who decimates and then lightly smooths recovers the same warning time. The wavelet denoiser drops to 6/12 valid alarms, below even raw decimation, because soft-thresholding the detail coefficients perturbs the pre-onset baseline enough to lift the pre-onset false-alarm rate past the budget: smoothing that preserves the normal baseline recovers the lead time, while smoothing that distorts it can cost validity. The one-alarm gap between raw decimation (8/12) and aggregation (7/12) at statistically indistinguishable lead times (76.0 vs.\\ 75.6~h) is sampling noise at $n=12$, not evidence that decimation is preferable.")
P("On genuine 10~s industrial SCADA data ending in a real operator shutdown,",
  "Nine of the eleven detectors alarm 30--35~h before the labeled failure (seven within 34.9--35.3~h, CUSUM at 33.9~h and Hotelling $T^2$ at 30.4~h), the naive RMS-trend baseline gives 22.8~h, and Deep SVDD does not alarm before failure. The aggregate-vs-decimate difference is at most about a minute for every detector (Table~\\ref{tab:imsongc}): on data that already is a real historian stream, the choice of logging mechanism does not materially change warning time, which is the cleanest possible illustration of the practical question and consistent with ``averaging is not the enemy.'' Because ONGC is a single asset ($n=1$), we treat it as a descriptive case study and draw no statistical inference from it. Its conformal calibration (Figure~\\ref{fig:conformal}d) tracks the diagonal closely, consistent with a mostly-stationary normal region.")
d.save()
